To achieve the effect similar to `SELECT *, 'bar' AS Foo FROM items` in LINQ for an anonymous type, you need to explicitly include each property of the `item` object in the creation of the new anonymous type. Unfortunately, C# does not allow you to automatically unpack an object into an anonymous type directly. This means you have to manually specify each property you want to include from the `item`, along with any additional properties you wish to add.

If your `item` objects come from a known class with a fixed set of properties, you can directly map these properties. Here's a simplified example assuming `item` is of a class `Item` with properties `Id` and `Name`:

```csharp
var newItems = new List<object>(); // Using object here, but in real code, you might want a more specific type or another anonymous type

foreach (var item in items)
{
    var newItem = new {
        item.Id,
        item.Name,
        Foo = "bar"
    };

    newItems.Add(newItem);
}
```

This code snippet manually includes each property (`Id` and `Name`) from `item` in the creation of `newItem`, along with the new property `Foo`.

However, if the properties of `item` are not known at compile time, or if `item` has a dynamic set of properties (e.g., coming from a dynamic object or a dictionary), you'll face a challenge because C# requires knowing the property names at compile time for this approach.

For dynamic objects or for cases where the properties are not known until runtime, you might need to use reflection to dynamically read the properties of `item` and construct an object with these properties plus your additional ones. However, doing so with anonymous types is complex and generally not recommended. Instead, you might consider using a `Dictionary<string, object>` or a similar structure to hold the properties, though this loses some of the type safety and convenience of anonymous types.

Here's an example of how you might dynamically add properties to a dictionary for each item, which is not exactly what you asked for but illustrates handling dynamic properties:

```csharp
var newItems = new List<Dictionary<string, object>>();

foreach (var item in items)
{
    var itemProperties = item.GetType().GetProperties().ToDictionary(
        prop => prop.Name,
        prop => prop.GetValue(item)
    );

    // Add the new property
    itemProperties.Add("Foo", "bar");

    newItems.Add(itemProperties);
}
```

This approach uses reflection to get and copy the properties of each `item` into a dictionary and then adds the new property. It's more flexible but sacrifices some performance and type safety compared to directly mapping properties in an anonymous type.

