The following code implements a Radius Neighbors Classifier in R using a custom non-Euclidean distance metric with the option to select k-nearest neighbors based on the calculated distances.

```R
# Define the RadiusNeighborsClassifier function
RadiusNeighborsClassifier <- function(trainX, trainY, testX, radius, distFunc, k) {
  # Check if arguments are valid
  if(!is.function(distFunc)) stop("distFunc must be a function")
  if(length(trainX) != length(trainY)) stop("trainX and trainY must have the same length")
  if(radius <= 0) stop("radius must be greater than 0")
  if(k <= 0) stop("k must be greater than 0")

  # Initialize predictions vector
  predictions <- c()

  # Iterate over each test point
  for(i in 1:nrow(testX)) {
    # Calculate distances to all training points
    distances <- sapply(1:nrow(trainX), function(j) distFunc(trainX[j,], testX[i,]))

    # Identify neighbors within radius
    neighbors <- which(distances <= radius)

    # If no neighbors within radius, skip this test point
    if(length(neighbors) == 0) {
      next
    }

    # Get classes of neighbors
    neighborClasses <- trainY[neighbors]

    # Sort neighbors by distance, and take top k
    sortedNeighbors <- sort(neighborClasses)[1:min(k, length(neighborClasses))]

    # Predict the most common class among the k nearest neighbors
    prediction <- names(which.max(table(sortedNeighbors)))

    # Add prediction to results
    predictions <- c(predictions, prediction)
  }

  return(predictions)
}
```

This function takes as input a training set (`trainX` and `trainY`), a test set (`testX`), a radius (`radius`), a distance function (`distFunc`), and a number of neighbors to consider (`k`). Note that `distFunc` must be a function that takes two vectors and returns a single scalar value - a measure of distance between them.

For each test point, it calculates the distance to every training point using `distFunc`. It then identifies the training points within the specified radius and selects the `k` closest ones. If there are no training points within the radius, it skips this test point. Finally, it predicts the class of the test point based on the classes of its `k` nearest neighbors (majority vote).

Please note that due to the nature of the Radius Neighbors Classifier, it may not perform well on high dimensional data, as it does not consider the structure of the data space. Therefore, it is recommended to use it only when the dimensionality of the data is low.

