Yes, you can prevent this from happening by using the `downloadReady` callback function in Tabulator. This function will be called just before the file is downloaded to allow you to modify the data that is being downloaded.

Here's an example of how you can use it:

```javascript
var table = new Tabulator("#example-table", {
    downloadReady: function(fileContents, blob){
        //do something with the file contents if required

        //return the modified file contents
        return fileContents;
    },
});
```

In the example above, the `downloadReady` function is passed two arguments: `fileContents` which is the string of all the table data, and `blob` which is the Blob object that will be used to initiate the download.

If you don't modify the `fileContents` or return a different value in the `downloadReady` function, Tabulator will use the original table data, and hence no refresh will take place.

Note: This will not stop the actual download or refresh of the page, but rather only affect the data that gets sent to the download.

