Proxying involves configuring your Angular application to send requests to a proxy server, which then forwards the requests to the actual API server. The proxy server can be configured to listen on a different port than the API server, so that the API server's ports do not need to be exposed to the internet.

There are several ways to set up proxying in an Angular application, but one common approach is to use the `proxy` option in the `nginx` configuration file. Here's an example configuration:
```markdown
http {
    ...
    upstream backend {
        server localhost:8080;
    }

    server {
        listen 80;
        server_name example.com;

        location / {
            proxy_pass http://backend;
            proxy_set_header Host $host;
            proxy_set_header X-Real-IP $remote_addr;
        }
    }
}
```
In this example, the `upstream` block defines the backend server as `localhost:8080`. The `server` block listens on port 80 for incoming requests, and then forwards the requests to the backend server using the `proxy_pass` directive. The `proxy_set_header` directives set the `Host` and `X-Real-IP` headers of the proxied request to pass along information to the backend server.

You can also use a reverse proxy module like `ngx_http_proxy_module` in your nginx configuration file.
```markdown
