One way to avoid memory leaks is to always `free` any memory you allocate as soon as you're done with it. However, if you forget to free memory, you'll end up with a memory leak. This can cause your program to consume more and more memory over time, leading to crashes or significant slowdowns if your machine runs out of memory.

In modern C, there are also smart pointers (e.g., `std::unique_ptr` in C++ or `autorelease` in Objective-C) which automatically free the memory when they go out of scope, making memory management easier and safer.

