Here's the code for the 'primeFib' method:

```java
import java.util.ArrayList;

public class Solution {

    public static int primeFib(int n) {
        if (n <= 0) {
            return -1;
        }
        int count = 0;
        int fib = 1;
        while (count < n) {
            fib = getNextFib(fib);
            if (isPrime(fib)) {
                count++;
            }
        }
        return fib;
    }

    private static int getNextFib(int n) {
        return n + 1;
    }

    private static boolean isPrime(int n) {
        if (n <= 1) {
            return false;
        }
        for (int i = 2; i <= Math.sqrt(n); i++) {
            if (n % i == 0) {
                return false;
            }
        }
        return true;
    }
}
```

The method first checks if the input 'n' is less than or equal to zero, in which case it returns -1. It then initializes a count variable to keep track of how many Fibonacci numbers have been found to be prime. It also initializes a fib variable to 1, which is the first Fibonacci number.

The method then enters a while loop that continues until the count reaches the desired value of 'n'. In each iteration of the loop, it gets the next Fibonacci number by calling the 'getNextFib' method, and checks if it is prime by calling the 'isPrime' method. If the number is prime, the count is incremented.

Finally, the method returns the last Fibonacci number found to be prime, which is the 'n-th' Fibonacci number.

