One of the most popular and commonly used sorting algorithms is the bubble sort. The working principle of bubble sort is as follows:

1. Start from the beginning of the array and compare each pair of adjacent elements.
2. If the first element is greater than the second element, swap them.
3. Continue this process for each pair of adjacent elements until the end of the array is reached.
4. Repeat steps 1-3 for the remaining unsorted elements until the entire array is sorted.

Here's the implementation of the bubble sort algorithm in Java:

```
public static void bubbleSort(int[] arr) {
    int n = arr.length;
    for (int i = 0; i < n - 1; i++) {
        for (int j = 0; j < n - i - 1; j++) {
            if (arr[j] > arr[j + 1]) {
                // Swap arr[j] and arr[j+1]
                int temp = arr[j];
                arr[j] = arr[j + 1];
                arr[j + 1] = temp;
            }
        }
    }
}
