    // Sub-allocate two chunks of memory
    void* ptr1 = pool.allocate(128);
    void* ptr2 = pool.allocate(256);

    if (ptr1 && ptr2) {
        std::cout << "Memory allocated successfully" << std::endl;
    }

    return 0;
}
```

In this example, the `MemoryPool` class manages a fixed-size memory block and provides methods to allocate and deallocate memory from this block. The `allocate` method returns a pointer to a chunk of memory of the specified size, while the `deallocate` method is a no-op since we don't support freeing individual allocations in this simple allocator.

While this approach allows you to sub-allocate memory from a larger block, keep in mind that managing memory manually like this can be error-prone and may lead to memory leaks or fragmentation if not handled carefully. It's generally recommended to use higher-level memory management facilities provided by the language or libraries whenever possible.

