One way to achieve this is by using a contentEditable div and wrapping the text in `<strong>` elements only when the user has finished typing. This will allow the cursor to remain at the end of the div and the text will be wrapped correctly.

Here's an example implementation using React:
```javascript
import React, { useState } from 'react';

function TextEditor() {
  const [text, setText] = useState('');
  const [isTyping, setIsTyping] = useState(false);

  const handleChange = (event) => {
    const content = event.target.innerText;
    if (content !== '-') {
      setText(content);
      setIsTyping(true);
    }
  };

  const handleBlur = () => {
    if (getText().trim() !== '') {
      const words = getText().split(' ');
      const htmlArray = words.map((word) => `<strong style="color: red">${word}</strong>`).join('');
      setText(htmlArray);
      setIsTyping(false);
    }
  };

  return (
    <div
      ref={node => {
        if (node) {
          node.focus();
        }
      }}
      contentEditable
      spellCheck="false"
      style={{ width: '400px', height: '200px', border: '1px solid black' }}
      onBlur={handleBlur}
      onInput={handleChange}
    >
      {getText()}
    </div>
  );
}
```
In this implementation, we use the `useState` hook to store the current text and whether the user is currently typing. We also define two event handlers: `handleChange` for updating the text when the user types, and `handleBlur` for wrapping the text in `<strong>` elements when the user finishes typing.

Inside the `TextEditor` component, we render a `div` that is set to be contentEditable. We also add event listeners for `onBlur` and `onInput` to handle changes to the text.

When the user starts typing, we set the `isTyping` state to true and update the `text` state with the new content. When the user finishes typing, we check if the text is not empty and wrap it in `<strong>` elements. We then set the `isTyping` state to false and update the `text` state with the new HTML content.

By wrapping the text in `<strong>` elements only when the user finishes typing, we ensure that the cursor position is preserved and the text is rendered correctly.

